# What survives when a paper leaves the lab: re-testing {{papers}} robot-learning papers on a €{{rig_eur}} rig, and auditing our own pre-registration

*Ravindu Somawansa, Shisso Robotics. Draft, not submitted.*

## Abstract

Most robot-learning claims are shown in simulation, on costly arms or on data-centre GPUs, and it is easy to assume they carry over to hardware a student can buy. On a €{{rig_eur}} rig (an SO-101 arm and a Jetson Orin NX), {{c_paper_repro}} of the {{c_paper}} paper claims we re-tested reproduce. Three proposed classes cover the failures: methods that miss a real-time budget on the edge GPU, zero-shot judges that a trivial signal beats, and latent planners that the commanded joints beat. We audited the study as well. Every published number was rebuilt from its stored rows, and commit order shows which bars came before their results (most did, not all). The classes wait for a blind second reader. So the advice is the one we followed: run the trivial baseline before crediting a learned method, and commit the bar before the result. All compute cost ${{cost}}.

## 1. The register

It is tempting to read a robot-learning paper as a statement about robots in general. Most are statements about one setup. Of the {{papers}} papers in this study, {{rig_sim}} were shown in simulation and {{rig_gt20k}} on rigs above $20k, against {{rig_5to20k}} at $5–20k and {{rig_lt5k}} below $5k ({{rig_unstated}} does not state its setup). Our rig is the other kind: an SO-101 arm and a Jetson Orin NX 16 GB, €{{rig_eur}} for the parts with a stated price ({{rig_unpriced}} small parts are not priced yet).

The register grew out of our own write-ups. Each re-test on this rig was published with its data ({{l0}} write-ups so far), and this paper collects them in one table, with an audit of how the table was kept.

One entry shows how the register works. An adaptive chunking paper claims that choosing the chunk length from the entropy of sampled chunks follows the task's phases (long in transport, short at the grasp). On our edge GPU one such decision takes {{ex_a_ours}} against a budget of {{ex_a_bar}}, so the entry records a fail, although the method may still work on the hardware it was shown on. Every entry has the same parts: the claim as the paper states it, the pass mark we fixed for our rig, our result, a verdict, and the published rows that recompute the number.

Two rules govern each entry. Before a learned model gets credit, the trivial signal it must beat (copy-last, the commanded joints, episode length) runs on the same rows, because a method that loses to it has not shown what it claims. And the pass mark is committed before the result, a rule whose record §2 audits.

We picked the papers for relevance to the stack this rig runs (latent world models, VLA policies, failure monitors and judges, action chunking, edge latency), and each went into the register before its test. The register file already lists the papers scheduled next.

## 2. Method, and an audit of our own pre-registration

Did each bar come before its result? Each gate of the experiment log has a bar and a result. For each, we find in git history the first commit that contains the bar and the first that contains the result, and classify the gate by commit ancestry alone (`git merge-base --is-ancestor`). Anyone with a clone can check ancestry, which is not true of a note or a timestamp, so we use neither. Of the {{pr_rows}} gate rows in the log on 2026-10-31, **{{pr_bar_first}} are bar-first** and {{pr_result_first}} result-first. The others are {{pr_same_commit}} same-commit, {{pr_no_bar}} with no bar and {{pr_not_run}} with a bar but no result yet. The log has since grown to {{pr_today}} rows ({{pt_bar_first}} bar-first, {{pt_same_commit}} same-commit, {{pt_result_first}} result-first, {{pt_no_bar}} no-bar, {{pt_not_run}} not-run). Same-commit means the bar and the result first appear in one commit of this repository, which says nothing about which came first.

Our first pass searched the text of the log and got a different picture: {{v1_bar_first}} bar-first, {{v1_same_commit}} same-commit, {{v1_no_bar}} no-bar and {{v1_unresolved}} unresolved. The log often received a bar and its result in one commit even when the bar had been written earlier in a protocol, plan, scorer or specification. So we traced each same-commit gate to that file. This moved {{sc_to_bf}} to bar-first and {{sc_to_nb}} to no-bar (their bar cell declares none), and left {{sc_stay}}. The audit table gives the evidence line for each upgrade. We did not re-audit the rows that were already bar-first for a result hidden elsewhere in the history. One gate added on 2026-11-01, the reward-model progress test, reads same-commit by text search although its protocol (`{{top_protocol}}`) predates the log commit. Its result commit is not audited yet, so it stays same-commit as an open candidate.

The early gates mixed point estimates with assorted intervals. We recomputed {{rc_rows}} of them from stored rows with one statistic, the episode bootstrap ({{rc_resamples}} resamples, percentile 95 % CI), and found {{rc_flips}} verdicts whose bar falls inside the interval. The judge accuracy is the clearest case: {{acc_est}} % on {{acc_n}} episodes against a bar of {{acc_bar}} %, a {{acc_verdict}} on the point estimate that an interval of {{acc_lo}}–{{acc_hi}} % cannot settle. The oscillation rate among {{osc_n}} failed episodes is a {{osc_verdict}} on the point ({{osc_est}} % against a bar of {{osc_bar}} %) with a lower bound of {{osc_lo}} %. A judge threshold's false-intervention rate, {{tau_est}} % on {{tau_n}} episodes against a ceiling of {{tau_bar}} %, is a {{tau_verdict}} whose interval reaches {{tau_hi}} % (and since the threshold was fitted to meet that rate, the interval is only descriptive). The register keeps the point verdicts and marks these three as fragile.

Every published number was recomputed from its published rows, and all {{l0}} of {{l0}} write-ups pass. Rerunning cold is harder. From a fresh clone with no credentials, {{l1_repro}} gates rerun and reproduce, {{l1_exact}} of them exactly. Another {{l1_env}} are blocked by one software environment we never published, and the remaining {{l1_skip}} need a larger GPU, a private checkpoint, the arm or a step the write-up leaves out. We also checked the register's citations and paper-side fields against the papers' PDFs and merged the fixes. Of the {{ps_ids}} arXiv ids the published register names, those missing from our papers mirror belong to scheduled tests. One tested paper has no arXiv id and was not checked against its PDF.

