Counted by paper, {{p_reproduces}} of the {{papers}} reproduce and {{p_fail}} fail (Table 1), while {{p_not_proven}} are not proven, {{p_not_tested}} was not tested and {{p_withdrawn}} was withdrawn. The register holds {{claims}} claim rows, {{claims_tested}} of them tested. {{c_ours}} of these rows test our own baselines rather than a paper's claim, for example "the commanded joints beat the world model", and {{c_ours_repro}} of those reproduce. On the {{c_paper}} claims taken from papers, {{c_paper_repro}} reproduce and {{c_paper_fail}} fail. Over all rows the count is {{c_reproduces}} reproduce, {{c_fail}} fail, {{c_not_proven}} not proven, {{c_not_tested}} not tested and {{c_withdrawn}} withdrawn.

## 4. Three failure classes

What beat the method? We propose three classes for the {{fail_rows}} failed claim rows, named after the answer. In class A it is the clock: the method may work, but on the edge GPU it misses the real-time budget the claim needs, like the adaptive chunking entry of §1 ({{ex_a_ours}} per decision against {{ex_a_bar}}). Class B is zero-shot judges, models used without training on this rig to judge success, failure or progress, that lose to a trivial signal. A learned reward model detects failures at AUROC {{ex_b_ours}}, where episode length alone reaches {{ex_b_len}}. Class C is learned latents used to plan, rank or track progress that do worse than the commanded joints or a copy-last or action-only baseline. A latent world model ranks the executed chunk first in {{ex_c_ours}} of anchors, below its bar of {{ex_c_bar}} and below the {{ex_c_base}} of an action-only baseline.

These examples illustrate the classes and count nothing. Under the protocol committed before any reading, per-row labels count only once a blind second reader, labelling the same {{fail_rows}} rows on the same form, agrees with the first pass at Cohen's κ ≥ {{kappa_bar}}. The protocol and the form are in the repository, and the second reading has not happened yet.

## 5. Limitations

The weakest point is that one person stands behind every verdict. The author ran the rig and labelled the outcomes, at rest but not always blind to the arm, and no second person has checked a verdict or a class yet. The class reading of §4 is the first such check, and its protocol is committed. Order is the next concern. For {{pr_unprov}} of the {{pr_rows}} gates ({{pr_same_commit}} same-commit, {{pr_no_bar}} with no bar) this repository cannot show that the bar came first, and the {{sc_to_bf}} upgrades of §2 depend on matching a bar text. Under the bootstrap {{rc_flips}} verdicts are fragile, and only {{l1_repro}} gates have been rerun cold (most of the rest wait on the unpublished environment). Everything comes from one rig, so a failure here is a finding about this rig and not a refutation of the paper in its own setting. The papers were chosen for relevance to one stack, which means the reproduction rate describes this register and not the field.

## 6. Related work

Work on the same arm evaluates policies. A standardized real-world benchmark reports a taxonomy of failures and recoveries for VLA policies [@2606.08881], and a fleet of SO-101 cells evaluates policies at scale [@2607.24481]. The vla-eval tool runs VLAs across 14 simulation benchmarks [@2603.13966], and a benchmark of demonstration quality studies one method family empirically [@2610.18869]. We test something else: the claims behind methods (judges, world models, latency) against bars fixed before the run, with our own pre-registration audited. The closest statement of our thesis [@2610.40026] scores VLA and world-action models on five dimensions, among them reproduction evidence and deployment affordability. Its reproduction-evidence score counts five audit milestones (configuration mapping, task records, task coverage, training records, seeds), and its authors state that it is not a probability that a result is correct. We give one pass or fail per claim on a €{{rig_eur}} rig instead.

## 7. Conclusion

On a €{{rig_eur}} rig, {{c_paper_repro}} of {{c_paper}} tested paper claims reproduce under bars fixed for this hardware before the run. Compute was cheap (${{cost}} for all of it). The hard part was order, keeping every bar ahead of its result, and our own history has {{pr_unprov}} of {{pr_rows}} gates where we cannot prove we did. For anyone re-testing papers on a cheap rig, these are the three habits we would keep: run the trivial baseline on the same rows first, commit the bar before the run, and publish the rows so the number can be rebuilt. Every number here can be rebuilt from the repository (`github.com/ravediamond/paper-register-reproducibility`).

